# Commit as you go

When you change this project, commit immediately. Do not wait for a separate request, and do not batch several edits into one commit at the end.

- Commit after every small change, as soon as that change is done and checked. A one-line tweak, a copy edit, a style adjustment, and a bug fix each get their own commit.
- Do not hold a finished change until related work is also done. If two things can be described separately, commit them separately.
- Check the change first (tests, lint, or the relevant verification), then commit before starting the next change.
- Write a concise message that says why the change was made.
- Follow the git safety protocol: no force push, no skipped hooks, no git config changes, and no secrets.
- Push only when asked.
